From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Wed, 26 Oct 1994 10:11:23 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-10-512] Brain backup proposal

Jason  Hamilton wrote:

>1)  A lot of of informations processing goes on in other parts of the 
>body than the brain.  The processing of sensory information and 
>"muscular" or reflex memory starts in the neurons in the spinal cord.  
>Your nanobots ought to be able to attach to these as well.

A reasonable, identity-preserving upload process have to involve the body
too, although not necessarily in as great detail as the brain. 
Fortunately, most of the peripherous nervous system is much simpler (and
bigger) than the central system, so measuring it will be almost a piece of
cake compared to the brain. Exactly how much of the body has to be
measured and simulated is an open question. We want to get roughly the
same "feel" of the body as before, but this doesn't mean we have to 
simulate every little muscle-fibre. The description in Permutation City 
about how the body-simulation approximates reality was quite 
enlightening, essentially it doesn't have to be that close as long as the 
results are close enough (i.e. very simple simulations of digestion and 
nutrient uptake, ray-tracing vision etc). 

And yes, I do believe that a body simulation is necessary for an upload 
at first. Our minds are very tied into a certain way to experience things 
(a constant stream of information through the body), and a drastic 
change would probably be very traumatic. Its better to slowly 
learn to manage without a body than suddenly being a disembodied being. 
As time goes on, the body-image could be gradually be altered, ending in 
a more flexible state. 


>2) Much of what we experience is determined by a lot more than when a
>neuron fires.  The brain is not an electronic computer (binary or
>otherwise).  Neurotransmitters are generated by different glands in both
>cortex, and slosh freely around the brain.  A lot of information
>processing, espescially memory, is directly related to the particular
>balance of these chemicals.  

Not to mention internal cell-states. Apparently certain aspects of memory 
result from various genetic switches changing state (I think this might 
be the reason older brains are less flexible, many cells have gone into a 
"read only mode" as they have matured). 

In general, taking backups of a living brain without destroying or
altering it unduly seems to be quite hard. But on the other hand, the
payoffs (like true immortality, increased flexibility, energy-efficiency)
seems to be so large that its worth it. 

