At 11:31 PM 10/25/94, Tim Starr wrote:
>Another couple of sources worth looking into are "Generations: The History
>of America's Future," by Strauss & Howe, and "Generation X: Abort? Retry?
>Fail?" by the same authors, although I haven't read the latter.  The former
>elaborates their cyclical theory of history: there are 4 different kinds
>of generations, which repeat each other in roughly the same order, although
>one kind may get skipped now and then.  Among the fascinating implications
>of their theory is that the same arrangement between generations that
>produced both the Glorious Revolution in England in 1688 and the American
>Revolution in 1776 is about to come up again right around 2004-5.  Ready?

We have exact opposite experience with Strauss & Howe; I've read
_Generation X_ and not the other. As far as Gen X is concerned, I
think the book is pure garbage. The origin of the book is telling;
it was originally written for a target reader over age 30, but they
couldn't sell it in that form, so it was rewritten for consumption
by the 'slacker' element. They added in impossible dialog with a
self-proclaimed slacker over a bbs of some sort (who now defends the
book over in alt.society.generation-x), hipper graphics and slightly
different conclusions.

All in all, it is a book written to 1) make people who identify with
the generation X marketing concept feel OK about who they are, and
2) people who think there is a problem with people who identify with
the concept feel OK about thinking that. Everybody leaves feeling warm
and fuzzy. The book has no real substance. Strauss & Howe did very
selective research to reach their vapid conclusions, skipping over
anything that doesn't fit. (One of my favorite conclusions was that the
Reagan administration gave everyone in the 18-30 age group hopeless angst
and bitterness about the US economy. One can only guess they couldn't find
any Young Republicans to interview.)

Getting past bashing this book, my take on Generation X is it is
a great marketing niche, but not much else. Imagine the press in
the '60s figuring out that hippies would have been a great market
segment to cater to, and update that image for today. In the most
recent International Design magazine, there is a good introduction
to marketing to slackers, if anyone is interested.

